"A software supply-chain provenance graph traces a released artifact to its source, dependencies, builder, and attested build process.\n\nUse when: Review the origin of a deployable artifact. Explain which evidence links a release to trusted build inputs.\n\nAvoid when: The objective is only task scheduling without artifact identity.\n\nVisual grammar: Nodes distinguish source revisions, dependency artifacts, build runs, and outputs. Derivation edges identify which inputs produced each output. Attestation nodes or annotations identify issuer, subject digest, and verification status.\n\nBuild: Start from an immutable output digest. Trace declared build inputs and the builder identity. Attach provenance evidence and distinguish verified statements from unverified claims. Lay out derivation left to right with evidence in separate callouts.\n\nCheck: Artifact identity uses digests rather than mutable tags alone. An attestation’s subject matches the artifact under review. Signature presence is distinguished from trusted verification. Evidence callouts and derivation arrows leave artifact labels unobstructed.\n\nPitfalls: Treating a dependency list alone as complete build provenance. Assuming signed provenance proves the build was free of vulnerabilities.\n\nExample: A container digest links to a build run, source commit, dependency inputs, and an explicitly verified provenance statement.\n\nAlternatives: Artifact promotion diagram, Container image layer diagram."
